\DOC WHILE_TAC

\TYPE {WHILE_TAC : bool ref -> tactic -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Repeatedly applies a tactic for as long as a boolean reference flag remains
set, leaving a single transformed goal.

\DESCRIBE
{flag : bool ref} is a mutable boolean tested between iterations; the supplied
tactic may clear it to terminate the loop. {tac : tactic} is the tactic run on
each iteration. The combinator runs {tac} on the current goal over and over
while {flag} holds, yielding exactly one goal.
{
       A ?- t
     ==================  WHILE_TAC flag tac
       A' ?- t'
}
\noindent In the observed traces, the conclusion {t'} is {t} structurally
unchanged except that its final argument is replaced by a later term introduced
during the iterations. The assumption list is extensively rewritten:
assumptions phrased in terms of the original argument are discharged and
replaced by corresponding ones about the new term, together with further
derived assumptions — extra equations about state components, a combined
accumulated-change assumption relating the original and new terms, and a side
condition about an abbreviated term. Assumptions unrelated to the changing term
are re-asserted unchanged, so {A'} contains both the preserved part of {A} and
the new assumptions.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-goal, assumption-rewriting outcome,
always on goals of the same shape and with a loop body that performs
incremental stepping.

\ENDDOC
